{
  "sn41.6:0.1": "Linked Discourses 41.6 ",
  "sn41.6:0.2": "1. With Citta ",
  "sn41.6:0.3": "With Kāmabhū (2nd) ",
  "sn41.6:1.1": "At one time Venerable Kāmabhū was staying near Macchikāsaṇḍa in the Wild Mango Grove. ",
  "sn41.6:1.2": "Then Citta the householder went up to Venerable Kāmabhū, sat down to one side, and said to him: ",
  "sn41.6:1.3": "“Sir, how many processes are there?” ",
  "sn41.6:1.4": "“Householder, there are three processes. ",
  "sn41.6:1.5": "The physical process, the verbal process, and the mental process.” ",
  "sn41.6:1.6": "Saying “Good, sir,” Citta approved and agreed with what Kāmabhū said. Then he asked another question: ",
  "sn41.6:1.7": "“But sir, what is the physical process? What’s the verbal process? What’s the mental process?” ",
  "sn41.6:1.8": "“Breathing is a physical process. Placing the mind and keeping it connected are a verbal process. Perception and feeling are a mental process.” ",
  "sn41.6:2.1": "Saying “Good, sir,” he asked another question: ",
  "sn41.6:2.2": "“But sir, why is breathing a physical process? Why are placing the mind and keeping it connected a verbal process? Why are perception and feeling a mental process?” ",
  "sn41.6:2.3": "“Breathing is physical. It’s tied up with the body, that’s why breathing is a physical process. ",
  "sn41.6:2.4": "First you place the mind and keep it connected, then you break into speech. That’s why placing the mind and keeping it connected are a verbal process. "